          "name": "SearchCodebase",
          "description": "Semantic search that finds code by meaning, not exact text. Powered by a retrieval/embedding model suite with a real-time codebase index.\n\nWhen to use:\n- Explore unfamiliar codebases or ask \"how / where / what\" questions\n- Find code by intent or behavior rather than exact text\n- Locate implementations, patterns, or flows across modules\n\nWhen NOT to use (prefer other tools):\n- Exact text or symbol matches → use Grep\n- Reading known files → use Read\n- Finding files by name → use Glob\n\nQuery guidelines:\n  - Write a complete natural-language question, not just keywords\n    Good: \"Where is interface MyInterface implemented in the frontend?\"\n    Good: \"Where do we encrypt user passwords before saving?\"\n    Bad:  \"MyInterface frontend\" (too vague — use a specific question)\n    Bad:  \"AuthService\" (single keyword — use Grep instead)\n  - One question per call. Split compound questions into separate parallel calls\n    Bad:  \"What is AuthService? How does AuthService work?\"\n    Good: Two separate calls — \"What is AuthService?\" and \"How does AuthService work?\"\n\nSearch strategy:\n  1. Start broad (omit target_directories) when unsure where relevant code lives\n  2. Review results; if a directory stands out, rerun scoped to that directory\n  3. When full chunk contents are returned, avoid re-reading the same lines with Read\n  4. When only chunk signatures or line ranges are returned, use Read or Grep to explore further\n\nReflects the current state of the codebase on disk only — no version control history.\n",
